      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "as of this date, `VRCPlayerApi.GetPlayers` still includes the *leaving* player when called in the **same frame** as `OnPlayerLeft` event.\n\nas stated by **Reimajo**, this is indeed counterintuitive given that the event name is `OnPlayerLeft` and not `OnPlayerLeaving` which causes the confusion.\n\nfor those looking for a simple workaround, I found that, after hours of testing, delaying the invocation of `VRCPlayerApi.GetPlayers` by at least one frame (relative to `OnPlayerLeft`) would yield the expected players (i.e.: all but the one *leaving*).\n\nhere's a snippet for convivence:\n\nprivate VRCPlayerApi[] Players;\n\nprivate void Start() => Players = new VRCPlayerApi[32]; // replace 32 with actual max capacity.\n\npublic override void OnPlayerLeft(VRCPlayerApi Player) => SendCustomEventDelayedFrames(nameof(MyCustomEvent), 1);\n\n// the method has to be declared public\n\npublic void MyCustomEvent() {\n\n    int PlayerCount = VRCPlayerApi.GetPlayerCount();\n\n    for (int Index = 0; Index < PlayerCount; ++Index)\n\n        Debug.Log(Players[Index].displayName);\n\n}",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2021-02-27T14:59:45.310Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "I see a lot of people stumble upon this issue and everyone else is using their own solution instead of using VRCPlayerApi.GetPlayers() which in theory is a great function, but fails when you want to keep a list of all players up to date.\nThat's simply because when using OnPlayerLeft(), the VRCPlayerApi.GetPlayers() still contains the player that left which is counter-intuitive and means you can't keep a list of all players up to date by just calling this function in OnPlayerJoined() and OnPlayerLeft() to update your player array. And since everyone is expecting this to work like that, they quickly run into this issue.",
